## Findings

### High — Endpoint reinspection mishandles disappearance and appearance races

`probeManagedRuntime()`'s `markerResult(socket)` treats a previously inspected socket that disappears as “runtime socket changed” and throws. The package explicitly requires disappearance after a no-listener result to become `missing` (or marker-derived `starting`/dead-marker cleanup), while only a replacement identity fails conservatively.

The inverse race is also unsafe: when the initial socket is missing but `inspectManagedRuntime()` sees a socket appear, `markerResult(undefined)` ignores that socket and can return `missing` or stale-marker cleanup. It must not report absence or grant cleanup while a new endpoint has appeared. Classify this conservatively or restart only the classification logic permitted by the package's one-pass policy, and add deterministic race evidence.

### High — Managed runtime diagnostics lose their tagged path at the server boundary

Managed directory failures are mapped with `socketError("prepare", cause)`. `MusicSessionSocketError` has no `path`, and `formatDaemonError()` reads only the outer error, so the executable reports the operation/message but drops the `MusicSessionRuntimeError` path and tag. The package requires runtime/path failures to retain tagged operation, path, and message diagnostics with nonzero status.

Preserve the runtime error at this boundary or carry its path through the server error, and exercise the real no-flag executable graph against an unsafe managed directory.

